Z||000000||CozmoPooky||12-23-2000||08:03 PM||||Just read Gary Witzenburg's Fiero book and it seems that an over-the-roof engine air inlet was considered by Pontiac designers from day one.  How to do it with a V-8 (Archie or 4.9 Caddy) that's functional and asthetic?<BR>Maybe even get rid of them louvers...<BR>Every time I walk by the car I want to throw a burger on... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>CozmoPooky<BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>'87 Duke Coupe<BR>'85 2M6 Targa||||reg||10
Z||000001||Philphine||12-23-2000||09:20 PM||||that was a minor thing i didn't like about a small block v8 swap. i'd have to go with fuel injection to pass the vet and the tuned port inlet is about in the wheelwell. have to get some twisted air flow to route it from an indy scoop. but i was browsing through a car mag in the store and there was a featured car with tuned port that had the air inlet coming out the side. i read through the article but it didn't mention a company name. it looked like the answer though if a v8 swap opportunity came my way. <P>i think with the caddy 4.9 the air intake is close enough to the center that it should be easier to work up an inlet to the scoop. ||||reg||1
